# Normalise all text to LF in the repo, regardless of contributor OS.
# This is the source of truth the pre-commit `mixed-line-ending --fix=lf`
# hook enforces — without it, Windows checkouts can reintroduce CRLF and make
# the shell hook scripts fail with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Rust / Node / config — always LF.
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.jsonc     text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must stay CRLF or cmd.exe mis-parses them.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Lockfiles are text but noisy — keep LF, let git handle them.
Cargo.lock       text eol=lf
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# Binaries — never normalise or diff as text.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.webp      binary
*.db        binary
*.vsix      binary

# Append-only lists. Every concurrent branch adds an entry at the same place, so
# a textual conflict is guaranteed and the resolution is always the same: keep
# both. `merge=union` is git's built-in driver for exactly that — it takes both
# sides of a conflicting hunk instead of writing markers.
#
# CHANGELOG.md is deliberately NOT listed. Git honours this driver in a checkout;
# GitHub's merge machinery does not, so a changelog-touching pull request
# reported a conflict that did not exist and auto-merge silently went stale —
# five times in one day. Entries now live in `changelog.d/` as one file per
# change and are assembled at release (ADR-0056), so no branch edits a shared
# file and there is nothing to merge.
#
# docs/adr/README.md keeps the attribute. It is generated by
# scripts/adr-index.mjs and guarded by a hook, so a stale index is regenerated
# rather than merged; union is a convenience here, not the mechanism.
#
# DEVELOPERS.md is not listed either, and for a different reason: its prose gets
# revised in place, and union merge never reports a conflict — two branches
# rewording the same paragraph would silently keep both copies. A file whose
# lines change needs a human to look; a file whose lines only accumulate does
# not.
docs/adr/README.md  merge=union
